<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porto Torres</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Turris Libisonis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Torres is a port town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, the Roman colony of Turris Libisonis. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it gave its name to the Judicate of Torres (Logudoro), and its Romanesque basilica of San Gavino is the largest in Sardinia. The harbour served as the outlet for the trade of nearby Sassari.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porto-torres.jpg" alt="Porto Torres" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gianni Careddu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porto_Torres_-_Municipio_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Porto Torres lies on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, in the centre of the Gulf of Asinara, about 25 kilometres east of Cape Falcone and 15 kilometres from Sassari. It is a comune in the Metropolitan City of Sassari. The municipality is split into two parts of almost equal size: the town, with its industrial area and Roman ruins, on the flat Nurra plain, and the islands of Asinara and Isola Piana, which since 1997 form the Asinara National Park. Its full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Colonia Iulia Turris Libisonis, where Turris, tower, probably refers to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghe</a> or to Monte d'Accoddi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of human presence include the altar of Monte d'Accoddi, necropolises and, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and Nuragic ages, the necropolis of Su Crucifissu Mannu, where two skulls show signs of surgery, possibly for relieving migraines and tumours or for religious reasons.</p><p>The town was founded in the 1st century BCE as Colonia Iulia Turris Libisonis, apparently by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, and was the first Roman colony on the whole island. Pliny calls it the only colony on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in his time, which suggests that there had been merely a fort before. The main road across the island ran from Caralis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>, to Turris, and a second road ran along the west coast. Remains include a temple dedicated to Fortune, baths, a basilica, an aqueduct and a bridge. It was an episcopal see in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The Romanesque basilica of San Gavino, built between 1065 and 1080, honours three saints said to have been beheaded in 303 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>.</p><p>The city was inhabited until the 11th century, when most people moved to Sassari. In the Judicate of Logudoro its name became Torres. It was partly under Genoese hands until the early 15th century, when the Aragonese took it, and later it became part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Torres was separated from Sassari in 1842, when the area around the basilica joined the fishermen's community by the port to form Porto Torres. It was bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> on 18 April 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Porto Torres is the second biggest seaport on the island after Olbia. The basilica of San Gavino, the largest Romanesque church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, has two apses instead of the usual western facade and eastern apse, and its crypt holds several Roman sarcophagi; it was the main cathedral of the archdiocese of Sassari until 1441.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Porto Torres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Logudoro, also called Torres after the old Roman port of <b>Porto Torres</b>, covered the north-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-logudoro">Judicate of Logudoro</a></li><li><blockquote>Sassari lived from grain, livestock and trade through <b>Porto Torres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-sassari">Republic of Sassari</a></li></ul></section>
